---
<p>A specialised type makes a value’s useful operations part of the database. A range can answer whether two intervals overlap. A spatial point can participate in a distance predicate under a declared coordinate system. The type, its operators and its index support work together; storing the same characters as text does not provide that contract.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL includes many such types and can install more through extensions. That lets a relational schema keep its ordinary keys and transactions while expressing richer questions. It also creates dependencies: application tables may need an extension’s types, indexes may need its operator classes, and a restored server must supply compatible supporting files.</p>
<p>We will begin with a room reservation, then test proximity on an invented local grid. These are independent examples. The room belongs to the collection’s tea-class setting, but its reservations do not describe session S501’s capacity race. The spatial points are anonymous test data, with no claimed shop locations or regional distances.</p>

<h2 id="types-establish-operations">Types establish operations</h2>
<p>Ordinary types already do this work. A numeric value supports arithmetic; an instant supports temporal comparisons. A domain can reuse an underlying type while giving a named constraint to its values. An enum declares a particular set of labels. Arrays, composites and JSONB assemble values in different ways, without necessarily giving their components independent row identities.</p>
<p>Choose the meaning before the convenience of its spelling. A string containing two timestamps requires every query to parse and interpret it. Two typed columns can be a sound representation, but every overlap query must use both endpoints correctly. A range packages that interval and its boundary rules into one typed value.</p>
<p>Built-in range types need no extension. <code>tstzrange</code> uses timestamp-with-time-zone instants; <code>tsrange</code> uses zone-free calendar readings. Use the former when reservations occupy particular instants. The display zone remains a separate concern, as <a href="/learn/databases/relational/values/">Values, missing data and equality</a> explains.</p>

<h2 id="what-an-interval-includes">What an interval includes</h2>
<p>For room R1, choose 4 November 2026 and a reservation from 18:00 to 19:00 UTC. Write it as <code>[18:00,19:00)</code>: include its start and exclude its end. A following reservation starting at 19:00 can then meet it without overlap.</p>
<Sql label="Construct two adjacent intervals explicitly" code={`SELECT tstzrange(
         '2026-11-04 18:00:00Z',
         '2026-11-04 19:00:00Z', '[)'
       ) && tstzrange(
         '2026-11-04 19:00:00Z',
         '2026-11-04 20:00:00Z', '[)'
       ) AS overlaps;
-- false`} />
<p><code>&amp;&amp;</code> asks whether the ranges share any element. <code>@&gt;</code> asks containment. These operators describe interval meaning directly, whereas comparing starts alone cannot establish overlap. A reservation from 18:30 to 19:30 overlaps the existing one even though the starts are different.</p>
<p>Ranges can also be empty or unbounded. Those are useful values for some applications, but this room contract requires a non-empty, finite, half-open interval. Declare that policy. Merely requiring the range column to be non-missing leaves these other values available.</p>
<p>Multiranges represent several disjoint ranges as one value. They can suit availability containing a morning and an afternoon interval. They do not turn separate bookings with their own identities into one booking; choose whether you are storing one set-valued fact or several independent reservations.</p>

<h2 id="enforce-the-overlap-rule">Enforce the overlap rule</h2>
<p>An index can find overlapping reservations. A rule must also prevent conflicting writes. Two clients can both ask whether R1 is free before either inserts its reservation. The empty answer is not a lock on every future interval.</p>
<p>An exclusion constraint forbids pairs of rows for which all the declared comparisons hold. Here the room IDs must compare equal and the intervals must overlap. Built-in range GiST support supplies overlap; the supplied <code>btree_gist</code> extension adds suitable GiST equality support for text room IDs.</p>
<Sql label="Declare the complete room conflict" code={`CREATE EXTENSION btree_gist;

CREATE TABLE reservations (
  room text NOT NULL,
  during tstzrange NOT NULL,
  CHECK (
    NOT isempty(during)
    AND lower_inc(during) AND NOT upper_inc(during)
    AND NOT lower_inf(during) AND NOT upper_inf(during)
    AND isfinite(lower(during)) AND isfinite(upper(during))
  ),
  EXCLUDE USING gist (room WITH =, during WITH &&)
);`} />
<p>The lower and upper infinity tests reject absent bounds; <code>isfinite</code> also rejects explicit infinite timestamp values. Required columns keep missing values from escaping the intended pairwise rule. A reservation identity and any room foreign key would belong in the application’s fuller schema; this fragment isolates interval validity and conflict.</p>
<p>Try each proposal in Figure 1. Changing only the room changes the rule’s answer, even though the times still overlap.</p>
<Reservations />
<p>The worksheet accepted the adjacent R1 interval and an overlapping R2 interval, then refused R1’s overlapping proposal with SQLSTATE <code>23P01</code>. Without the exclusion constraint, the counterexample stored both conflicting R1 reservations.</p>
<p>A separate two-connection native check also tested the conflict while the first insert was uncommitted. The second writer waited on the first transaction’s outcome. After the first committed, the second received the exclusion error and one reservation remained. This is concurrent enforcement by the constraint, rather than two successful application preflight checks.</p>
<p>The rule covers one room’s non-overlap. It does not enforce a class capacity greater than one, instructor coverage or every cross-row scheduling rule. Those need their own declared model and protocol. Range syntax makes the interval precise; it does not infer the rest of the business.</p>

<h2 id="spatial-values-need-units">Spatial values need units</h2>
<p>PostGIS extends PostgreSQL with geometry, geography and associated functions and index support. A geometry point records coordinates in a spatial reference system. Distances on geometry use that system’s units. A geography distance instead uses a geodetic model and metre-based predicates. Decide which question and accuracy the application requires before choosing between them.</p>
<p>Longitude and latitude degrees are not metres. Nor does assigning a spatial reference ID transform coordinates from one system into another. <code>ST_SetSRID</code> labels a value; <code>ST_Transform</code> performs a supported coordinate transformation. An apparently plausible numeric answer can still have the wrong units.</p>
<p>The native example avoids real geography altogether. It declares a local Cartesian grid whose numbers represent metres, using <code>geometry(Point,0)</code>. SRID 0 identifies no geodetic reference system. Points A, B, C and D have coordinates <code>(300,400)</code>, <code>(600,800)</code>, <code>(0,1200)</code> and <code>(700,700)</code>. Ten thousand additional generated points are deliberately far away.</p>
<Sql label="Search an explicit local metre grid" code={`CREATE INDEX points_location
ON r57_spatial.points USING gist (location);

SELECT label
FROM r57_spatial.points
WHERE ST_DWithin(location, ST_Point(0,0), 750)
ORDER BY label;`} />
<p><code>ST_DWithin</code> asks whether the points are within the supplied distance and can make use of a suitable spatial index. Figure 2 separates the index’s bounding candidates from the precise distance test.</p>
<Spatial />
<p>A is exactly 500 grid metres from the origin, so it qualifies. D is inside the 750-by-each-direction bounding square but about 989.95 metres away, so distance rejects it. B and C are outside that box. The query returns only A.</p>
<pre aria-label="Extract of the observed PostGIS proximity plan"><code>{`Index Scan using points_location on points
  Index Cond: (location && st_expand(origin, 750))
  Filter: st_dwithin(location, origin, 750)
  Rows Removed by Filter: 1
  actual rows=1 loops=1`}</code></pre>
<p>This extract replaces the printed binary point constant with <code>origin</code> and omits costs and timings. The native plan used the GiST box condition to obtain candidates, then the distance filter rejected D. Reading <code>ST_Distance(location,origin) &lt;= 750</code> as mathematically similar does not establish the same indexed route. Verify the supported predicate and actual plan.</p>
<p>The observed distance is geometric straight-line distance in our grid. It says nothing about walking routes, road access or delivery time. A real collection-point application must declare its actual coordinate system and use a suitable distance definition; the collection’s logical region names cannot supply one.</p>

<h2 id="installation-has-two-layers">Installation has two layers</h2>
<p>An extension’s supporting files must exist in the PostgreSQL installation before a database can load it. These can include control files, SQL installation and update scripts, compiled libraries and other native dependencies. <code>CREATE EXTENSION</code> then installs and records the extension’s SQL objects in one database.</p>
<p>Files available on the host are not the same as an installed database extension. Inspect both:</p>
<Sql label="Separate available packages from installed database objects" code={`SELECT name, default_version, installed_version
FROM pg_available_extensions
WHERE name IN ('btree_gist', 'postgis');

SELECT extname, extversion, extnamespace::regnamespace
FROM pg_extension
ORDER BY extname;`} />
<p>Many extensions require privileged installation. A trusted extension can be installed by a role with the required database privilege; “trusted” is a specific extension flag, not a claim that arbitrary packages are harmless. A managed service may provide only selected versions or forbid particular installation options.</p>
<p>Use the supported package and PostgreSQL-major-version combination. Installing into a schema does not give that schema an isolated execution engine: extension functions still run in the database server’s processes, and native code can have the server process’s capabilities. Keep installation schemas and search paths controlled. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/access-control/">Roles, policies and privileged code</a> handles that authority boundary.</p>

<h2 id="restore-the-dependencies-too">Restore the dependencies too</h2>
<p>A logical dump knows which SQL objects belong to an extension. It normally records creation of the extension instead of embedding every member function and type definition. That keeps the dump manageable, but it does not package native libraries or prove the destination can load a compatible version.</p>
<p>Before restoring a schema that uses geometry, prepare the destination’s PostGIS installation and its prerequisites. Check the version and installation schema the restore will use. Creating whatever default happens to be available may change the environment from the one tested. Application tables, indexes, constraints and queries all form part of the compatibility check.</p>
<p>The owned native drill dumped the example database and restored it into a new database on the same private PostgreSQL 18.6 server. The restored copy reported <code>btree_gist 1.8</code> and <code>PostGIS 3.6.4</code>; its proximity query still returned one point and the protected room table still contained one reservation. This tests a same-host logical restore with those supporting files already installed. It does not test a different operating system, new extension release or server-major upgrade.</p>
<p>Record both the extension version and relevant library versions. The spatial run reported GEOS 3.15.0 and PROJ 9.9.0 with network access disabled. Neither a version string alone nor the existence of its control file establishes that the article’s exact predicate and index work; the native query and restore supplied that evidence.</p>

<h2 id="updates-and-removal-follow-dependencies">Updates and removal follow dependencies</h2>
<p>An extension update changes database objects through supported scripts, usually using <code>ALTER EXTENSION … UPDATE</code>. Installing a new host package and running the database update are related steps, but they are not interchangeable. Some packages also require additional upgrade procedures or index work. Use the extension’s documented path and test representative results before changing the service.</p>
<p>Keep that path separate from a PostgreSQL major upgrade. The target server needs compatible extension libraries, and the extension may impose an ordering on its own update. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/upgrades/">Upgrading and migrating PostgreSQL</a> owns the server route. A backup is useful only if the older environment and a supported recovery path remain available when needed.</p>
<p>Removal follows dependency direction. The reservations constraint depends on a GiST text operator class supplied by <code>btree_gist</code>. The native attempt to drop the extension under those dependencies was refused with <code>2BP01</code>. A geometry column similarly depends on PostGIS’s type.</p>
<p><code>CASCADE</code> is permission to remove dependent objects too; it is not an automatic migration to ordinary types. First replace or remove the application’s uses deliberately, including columns, views, functions and indexes, and verify their replacement behaviour. Then remove the extension when its dependants no longer require it.</p>
<p>The value of a specialised type is the precise operation it makes available. The operational cost is keeping that operation valid through installation, concurrency, backup and change. Treat both as part of the schema the application depends on.</p>

